    "narrative": "Our primary offensive drivers for today center on the advantageous matchups for **Taylor Ward** (71.46) and **Bryson Stott** (70.70). **Taylor Ward** benefits immensely from a favorable park factor and a platoon advantage against Lopez, while **Bryson Stott** is a high-upside play against Dollander; the latter is particularly interesting given the 94% stolen base success rate allowed by the opposing battery, providing a rare opportunity to hunt for the league\u2019s scarcest category. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains a staple in our lineup (68.14) despite facing a reasonably competent arm in Prielipp, as his daily floor remains the most consistent in our core.\n\nThe lineup construction hinges on maximizing platoon efficiency, though we remain wary of the risk posed by Chris Sale, which suppresses the value of **Will Smith** (44.75). The most difficult decision this morning was evaluating **Masyn Winn** (51.12) against our starters; despite his potential, he was squeezed out by the sheer volume of high-ceiling, platoon-advantaged bats like **Brendan Donovan** (65.51) and **Cal Raleigh** (62.61). **Masyn Winn** was the final man left on the bench, narrowly missing the cut because our current outfield configuration and middle infield utility spots offer a higher collective probability of multi-category contributions today.\n\nWe are monitoring several \"Lineup Pending\" statuses, specifically for **Luis Arraez**, **Matt Chapman**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, and **Will Smith**. While the wind-out conditions at the park provide a +5% boost for **Luis Arraez**, **Matt Chapman**, and **Jung Hoo Lee**, any shift in their batting order would necessitate a recalibration of their efficiency scores. The match against Chris Sale is a significant volatility risk for **Will Smith**, though his underlying xwOBA provides some defensive comfort. Ensure all game caps are monitored closely, as we favor starting only those with a projection exceeding the 40-point threshold.",
